#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134753} :activity/id #uuid "cb02e51b-dee7-54a8-8f76-914d0b42e66b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135082 :tutorial/content "How do we represent the double integral of a function over a non-rectangular region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n![](images/3a/3ac99dd8336c3a877fbf6ba63a3074a490b85c8a350adad00da423412dd60ee3.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg)  is the finite region enclosed by the lines ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg), ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg), ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg) and ![](images/b5/b5489c125683eb29c2cababe2e1b7094d6a16b89d09970c42af649df70d045e4.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/af0a1a74df8aec0d5189dc2402fc1840c25d4cd94d8861fa06110cae7b449843.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a *type I* region. \nTo determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/39/39629b33e25aed05bf9a5ca36180f984e7ff8f863c6966d47f60483d672a8af4.png)\n\nSo, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be defined as \n\n![](images/78/78cf2ea7f960497f7cc9beea602bf6ca0e82b74491f9f56cce76130d965d4a64.svg)\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/3c/3c423b0d423203621ab89258a6fff4cea190279b4dcc7170c8a7070bf1f85e24.svg)\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that, when we have a type I region, \n\n- the limits for ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) are functions of ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg)\n\n- the limits for ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) are constants, and\n\n- we integrate over the variable ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) before integrating over the variable ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c641021-bc5e-5b05-8a23-b198735ee5a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4261 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a5c014d-2a5f-5ef2-b451-9d6636c3241e" :step/math-academy-id 25905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133648 :question/id #uuid "51467bae-f181-53ba-adf2-1218dc16275f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7024"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445829}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"} :step/id #uuid "3edf61a9-ab06-533a-ad5f-906f95245bb3" :step/math-academy-id 25906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133649 :question/id #uuid "7f443df5-1c26-5af4-9541-e83522126197" :question/math-academy-id "e-7025"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482544}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "621aac0e-49f3-510c-b684-d0f438398280" :step/math-academy-id 25907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135083 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{x}^{2x} (x-2y) \\ \\mathrm{d}  y \\ \\mathrm{d}  x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type I region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $y,$ we treat $x$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $x.$ Once again, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $y$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(x-2y)\\text{ }dy]\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[xy-{y}^{2}]_{x}^{2x}\\text{ }dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x\\cdot (2x)-{(2x)}^{2}]-[x\\cdot (x)-(x)^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}]-[{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx & =-2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3698e7e3-4bc2-5db0-96b5-172131284b4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4262 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals With Variable Limits"} :step/id #uuid "59bc86e8-4dae-564b-ab2b-c8ac5309d3be" :step/math-academy-id 25908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133650 :question/id #uuid "b151be22-91a1-5303-b86d-4f9ba748f000" :question/math-academy-id "e-7026"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445830}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "9095d8fd-3ce2-5af6-bda4-4a49a820addf" :step/math-academy-id 25909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133651 :question/id #uuid "24dd94c8-2821-5614-9ccf-43c3656267f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7027"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411861}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "bb2a0a7b-340c-53a3-a510-9a559cc6d898" :step/math-academy-id 25911}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134020}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408133} :activity/id #uuid "74e0d3a9-0075-5d33-b25c-7e801fde9553" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d2a009-9a40-5d25-99e2-4f97af963231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "2c533ee8-f9db-5f4a-a560-8035da471b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0552d2-cfa6-5c1e-901c-be3cad62fa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321590} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec84ef-6655-53bf-9eb0-7897a3753db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322030} {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2db87a-5385-53da-8e27-89a6fe772ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39356ac1-0a31-5e6e-9a6f-6a96bbbbe55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "098028bf-3706-5127-9bb7-debd78e7b253" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100fac5f-cc12-56dc-9567-ee01010ba781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408119} {:answer/id #uuid "1377aca7-a017-58fe-8e23-256c042e11b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408120} {:answer/id #uuid "3aabdbf2-aad9-5028-8b69-34e437771408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408121} {:answer/id #uuid "41ea5617-4c16-5bdc-a231-c9432f86dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408122} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7f804-0816-5794-84e7-a2fe566f8c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408121} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ef2574-1c06-549a-a456-4b22b82491c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ffe5914e-4912-5ff3-9b15-3a1462960003" :question/math-academy-id "q-132285" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}]_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20fb188a-faf6-5a84-b51f-1b3b2dd89239"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ddc79-cbfd-59b5-9638-d178b71104a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348536} {:answer/id #uuid "4b79160f-37ab-5192-8514-437d9b279188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349138} {:answer/id #uuid "544819eb-b6d1-5a40-bb3a-d34d78eef696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349218} {:answer/id #uuid "a06fce6a-f06f-595a-9863-76102c8d8305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} {:answer/id #uuid "f06aa1a1-4329-543e-8ec1-01bf70f9f867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} :answer-field/id #uuid "08077c01-ccff-5e05-a424-ef1243bb7696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b8f9d362-d296-527d-b3eb-8711496d6537" :question/math-academy-id "q-81367" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines\n$x=e,$ $y=e$ and the curve $y={e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "597be508-6945-5d32-9627-435849b9418f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b726bfa-ba4c-5d91-9f6d-6a3764d8755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} {:answer/id #uuid "1594e17d-7011-5f7d-aa6a-12336ec25a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321322} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} {:answer/id #uuid "564621d9-f224-5fff-b159-cf3615e51a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f698ad-bda0-5b89-8175-c64721d84bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb45d35-7041-5bf4-8524-44adec58ddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ad4a8e76-c297-5cff-8131-747df131b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "780a6e09-0224-5a5e-9ae0-33ed21780a56" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f2f8c3-eca9-558a-ae7f-9a78208457ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408123} {:answer/id #uuid "647e13d7-fe24-5171-9f25-59459873bf42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408124} {:answer/id #uuid "93175eb3-2514-5cc0-a423-e088c96ac28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408125} {:answer/id #uuid "93b1f2e7-cf7b-5b8a-b5bb-c62e89e19fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408126} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3be0a4-83b9-54e6-81b8-88c21e425fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408126} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df51106-2408-5663-8451-c957471724da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3676ec52-02c6-5b83-a547-e552c5ceb042" :question/math-academy-id "q-81338" :question/problem "![](images/41/4121d975786de30f34e493e7ac49e5cdc8e66621e800321008ba4ba501fb818f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2},$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/ef/ef6450f795d0edea8a3217e28d7f1a966d5b1c62f9241826213de0485af5f860.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le -{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5dbc16c-f16e-55e5-b468-49e1c21fdfb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408135}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408118}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482654} :activity/id #uuid "d50c6341-afab-531b-95d7-80980e2f8edd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55a9b55-701b-54b8-9dc3-ff767eb9a16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267638} {:answer/id #uuid "b89fe4b4-ca5f-5b19-9c79-5f2ac46d2595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267800} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6a5ad1-907d-59c7-a9c8-ed050fde1508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267859} {:answer/id #uuid "e85875be-4289-5cdd-8c4f-d83917f81081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268207} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfb32be-1877-5c91-86e5-ee84568b6b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb0802c-c72d-5b84-a668-8e4527960b23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07938b36-7b86-5365-9a53-980eadb141d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-132276" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[\\,y\\,]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09dc735b-be54-5f55-a40c-b3de9cb3c25f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125b9147-6355-50f2-ba8c-748686fc87f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348607} {:answer/id #uuid "6df205c3-6135-5d2b-b3fd-a8bcf211e047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349456} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2bfe7d-c973-5e74-8dfd-79e2236afc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349700} {:answer/id #uuid "90178deb-05ed-554a-b6da-bcd3010a2bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349741} {:answer/id #uuid "f59a2f53-d71c-5744-ba75-d029ceccffcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350594} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92c3f4e-98f4-5231-9b54-c6264a5301cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "adc09746-e915-53ae-9b39-14d8d6a2a18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81774" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1d9c3ed-33de-5684-8d99-d7f5f9b0c7e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "357e7017-c759-53cd-bd18-3b9cac8a059e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348931} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb83aea-26c4-5042-8913-2833d34004fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349582} {:answer/id #uuid "89dc3879-a9e4-5291-a430-52520db7f2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{3-{x}^{2}}^{x+1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349697} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f30da7-1720-596a-b960-80e3393eccfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350448} {:answer/id #uuid "f54b2107-78b3-5b8f-b15e-e4e04bce393b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{3-{x}^{2}}^{x+1}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350448} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e294541-5f38-5092-8793-4eff75d982fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a289df3d-5c34-5bee-85db-98d71fbc43da" :question/math-academy-id "q-81340" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=3-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/73/7316d667d7308ac5ec0f5348055d3d0e7eb90b97abaa74f5693c3151dc3b8bb1.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{x}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/aa/aab6fb99694b518bfa9d200a75876c9e888ba1ab7d699464bc9caca360be775a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,x+1\\le y\\le 3-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e0bceb01-13f0-500a-a70e-d7c56dc36189" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ba8975f-1dcf-5982-82b5-4619f25392af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349440} {:answer/id #uuid "909c8869-f444-52b1-8e81-f3f7d8cd7ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349746} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd6c44c-c13b-51de-a23a-1e56014ae0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbc989-fd06-546f-a58e-5a71b4609f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349910} {:answer/id #uuid "d230d5cd-76aa-5b60-afae-1d4ccdb5bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef03a78-3898-53d7-a31f-076237c237e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bba1a6f7-479e-5c7c-a4a7-2bc8097d537b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81361" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-(1-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ed3c1020-e19e-5ebc-82db-39b373afe65a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eac9096-a6ad-5d5e-9517-5c0ac0728af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481809} {:answer/id #uuid "533ce4cc-1308-5319-806a-167ee6b29c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-x/2}^{0}{\\int }_{-2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481974} {:answer/id #uuid "a522044d-f1ef-55e8-b1ee-f64410c9b0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{5-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{0}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482166} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a488ab-7038-5a44-bd73-39ae0126a903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{5-{x}^{2}}^{-x/2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482250} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f6ba16-5e4c-5134-9ee4-51804e3e92c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482251} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dce35b7-572a-54b4-af44-152ecca7b2e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756" :question/math-academy-id "q-132221" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a68077b18f8c08fc7cd68889b6472373c7677bcfcb6f370abce1e93cf67c4594.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $x=0,$ and the curves $y=5-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}x & =5-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(2x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard the solution $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ since $x\\le 0$ in our region. Therefore, $x$-limits are $-2\\le x\\le 0.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/5b/5bafe1d8cd35eec83aba3a9a24f777340a09f824919d4cedc5879c774e30cf5e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le 5-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f943e97a-19e3-5eb0-b644-0fd2bcdae89f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482622}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481771}]}
